Subject: Compare Identical Strings By Pointer Before Their Bytes

What this does
  js_string_eq returns true when both operands are the same JSString, before the
  length test and the memcmp.

Why we need it
  Two references to the same JSString compared a buffer against itself. Interned
  literals share a JSString, so testing a property against a constant, or a key
  against the previous key, lands there routinely.

What it adds
  One pointer test, placed in js_string_eq rather than in an interpreter fast
  path: it costs nothing in JS_CallInternal's stack frame and serves every caller
  -- Array.prototype.indexOf/includes, Map and Set keys, js_strict_eq2 and the
  property paths. No admission test is needed, because pointer identity implies
  equal content.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -5806,6 +5806,8 @@
 }
 
 static bool js_string_eq(JSString *p1, JSString *p2) {
+    if (p1 == p2)
+        return true;
     if (p1->len != p2->len)
         return false;
     return js_string_memcmp(p1, p2, p1->len) == 0;
